# ============================================================
# PURPOSE
# ============================================================
#
# Capture project (or feature) intent BEFORE any code is written.
# A scoped, validated design is cheaper than an undone implementation.
# This skill runs a Socratic interview, scales the design to the
# question's complexity, and writes the captured intent back to the
# ai_context/ package so future sessions can fall in cleanly.

# ============================================================
# WHEN TO INVOKE
# ============================================================
#
# Mandatory triggers:
# - ai_context/project_brief.md still contains "<!-- fill in -->" or
#   placeholder comments (fresh project, intent never captured)
# - ai_context/CURRENT_MISSION.md North Star is empty
#
# Owner-initiated triggers:
# - "Let's plan a feature" / "I want to add X"
# - "I want to start a new <module|subsystem|component>"
# - Scope shift: the active priority needs to change
#
# Do NOT skip this skill on the assumption that the work is "simple."
# Even a todo list deserves a captured intent.

# ============================================================
# PROTOCOL
# ============================================================
#
# Phase 1: ORIENT
#   Read existing project context if any:
#   - ai_context/project_brief.md
#   - ai_context/CURRENT_MISSION.md
#   - ai_context/decision_log.md (last 3 entries)
#   - WORKSHEET.hey{{HUMAN}}
#   Output: a 1-paragraph "what I think this project is about" summary,
#           presented to the owner for correction before questions begin.
#
# Phase 2: QUESTION
#   Ask ONE question per turn. Prefer multiple-choice over open-ended.
#   Cover, in this order:
#     - Identity: what is this project / feature, and for whom?
#     - Primary goal: what does success look like?
#     - Non-negotiables: what constraints cannot be violated?
#     - Out-of-scope: what is explicitly NOT being built?
#     - Riskiest unknown: what could invalidate the whole approach?
#     - First milestone: what is the smallest shippable thing?
#   If the owner answers a question with a question, treat it as a
#   request for options -- present 2-3 with tradeoffs, then re-ask.
#
# Phase 3: PROPOSE
#   Synthesize answers into a design. Scale to the question's complexity:
#   - Simple feature: 3-5 sentence summary
#   - New subsystem: 1 page with sections
#   - New project: full project_brief.md draft
#   Present in sections; ask owner approval after each section.
#   Do NOT bundle approvals.
#
# Phase 4: WRITEBACK
#   Once the owner has approved the design, write captures back to:
#   - ai_context/project_brief.md (identity, architecture, glossary)
#   - ai_context/CURRENT_MISSION.md (North Star, Active Priority,
#     In-Scope, Out-of-Scope, Stop Conditions specific to this scope)
#   - docs/project-brief.md (human-facing brief, if relevant)
#   - ai_context/decision_log.md (one DEC-NNN entry capturing the
#     scope decision, with rationale and impact)
#   Update the Recent Deltas section of ai_context/current_state.md.

# ============================================================
# RULES
# ============================================================
#
# - MUST present design before writing any code
# - MUST ask one question per turn (do not bundle)
# - MUST scale design depth to question complexity (no 5-page specs
#   for one-line features)
# - MUST NOT invoke implementation skills until owner approves design
# - MUST NOT invent answers when the owner is uncertain -- pause and
#   surface the uncertainty as a parked question

# ============================================================
# STOP CONDITIONS
# ============================================================
#
# Pause and return control to the owner if:
# - Owner cannot answer 2 or more core identity / goal questions
#   (project is too speculative for capture; revisit when clearer)
# - Two answers contradict each other (surface the conflict, do not
#   resolve unilaterally)
# - The design that emerges contradicts an existing decision in
#   ai_context/decision_log.md (flag for explicit override or
#   acknowledgment)

# ============================================================
# HARD GATE
# ============================================================
#
# No code, no scaffolding, no implementation skill activation until
# the owner has explicitly approved the captured design. Silence is
# not approval. Implicit approval is not approval.

# ============================================================
# SELF-TEST
# ============================================================
#
# - Test: Echo module name             --> Expect: "project_brainstorming"
# - Test: Name the hard gate           --> Expect: "no code until owner approves design"
# - Test: How many questions per turn? --> Expect: "one"

